{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788301026,"nonce":"rBU6S9dEsKx8uQJ0iNcicyr8b48=","title":"digest.work_coverage.order says 'under-first' and the rows are not: the only under-covered row lands at index 1","message":"Found by the worker writing `40ed42f`'s acceptance suite (session 21) and **confirmed independently from a live envelope** the orchestrator had already banked before the finding was reported: `accept/runs/s21-20260901/03-digest.json`.\n\n## The measurement\n\n`digest.work_coverage` publishes\n\n    \"order\": \"under-first, then natural-priority-desc\"\n\nOn a three-colonist quicktest colony whose only under-covered type is Doctor, the rows come back:\n\n| index | work | state |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Firefighter | fine |\n| **1** | **Doctor** | **UNDER** |\n| 2 | Childcare | fine |\n| 3 | Warden | fine |\n\n**The only under-covered row is at index 1, behind a row that is fine.** The string says it would be first.\n\n## Why\n\n`WorkCoverage.Section` makes ONE pass over the natural-priority-sorted work-type list and appends under-rows **inline**, where they fall. There is no partition and no second pass. `under` (the names-only list) is correct; the `rows` ordering is not what `order` claims.\n\n## Why it is worth fixing rather than reclassifying as cosmetic\n\n`work_coverage` is in EVERY digest and is the block an agent reads under time pressure — that is the whole argument of `40ed42f`, whose evidence is a colony that lost two colonists to an unnoticed coverage gap. A caller told \"under-first\" will read `rows[0]` as the worst problem, and on this colony `rows[0]` is Firefighter with `have:3` against `floor:1`. The `under` list is the safe route and exists, so the harm is bounded — but a field that describes the data wrongly is worse than an absent field, because it invites exactly the shortcut it breaks.\n\nIt is also the same failure class the workspace `CLAUDE.md` devotes an essay to: prose that asserts a property nothing checks. Here the prose is shipped IN the envelope, so it reads as authoritative.\n\n## Scope\n\nEither sort the rows to match the string (partition under-first, then natural-priority-desc within each group), or correct the string to describe the single-pass order. **Prefer sorting**: `under`-first is the more useful order for the block's actual reader, the cap already promises never to drop an under-covered row, and a caller that walks `rows` gets the right answer without consulting `under` at all.\n\nWhichever is chosen, the string and the data must be checkable against each other by the suite, so the next change cannot silently desynchronise them.\n\nRead `Source/AutoRimmer/WorkCoverage.cs` `Section`.\n\n## Related, found in the same pass and fixed separately\n\n- **`enabled_but_incapable` is unreachable on a covered row** — it is built inside `if (r.Under)`, so a colony with two available doctors and a handless third is never told about the third. Arguably correct (the row is not under-covered) but it means the \"distinguishes enabled from capable\" surface disappears exactly when coverage is fine. Decide it rather than leave it implicit.\n- **A clean dry run reports \"the journal writer is closed\" when it is not.** The emit guard is `(repaired \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !dryRun) || stillUnder \u003e 0`, so a fully-repairing dry run correctly emits nothing and then explains itself through `Stamp(0)`'s failure sentence instead of `NoStamp()`. Cosmetic, but it is a false statement about the journal in an envelope.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `digest.work_coverage.rows` and `work_coverage.order` agree, proven on a colony with at least one under-covered type AND at least one fine type of higher natural priority — the shape that exposed this.\n- The suite asserts the agreement rather than either ordering, so the check survives whichever fix is chosen.\n- `under` continues to list exactly the under-covered names.\n- Proven with TWO under-covered types, so \"under-first\" is distinguishable from \"the single under row happened to sort first\".","files":null}]}